      "completion_tokens": 1113,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule has a **bent (V) shape** (approx. 104.5° angle). Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion) via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant bonding is responsible for almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States (Phases)\nWater is one of the few substances that exists naturally on Earth in all three states:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Freezes at **0°C (32°F)** at standard pressure. Uniquely, ice is **less dense than liquid water** (it expands ~9% upon freezing), causing it to float. This insulates bodies of water, allowing life to survive underneath.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** Exists between 0°C and 100°C. It has a high **surface tension**, high **specific heat capacity** (resists temperature change), and high **heat of vaporization** (takes lots of energy to boil).\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Boils at **100°C (212°F)** at standard pressure. It is a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Anomalous Properties (Why Water is \"Weird\")\nWater behaves differently than almost any other liquid due to hydrogen bonding:\n*   **Density Maximum at 4°C:** Liquid water is densest at 4°C, not at its freezing point. This causes lakes to freeze from the top down.\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Its polarity allows it to dissolve more substances than any other liquid (salts, sugars, acids, bases, gases), facilitating chemical reactions and nutrient transport.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** It can absorb massive amounts of heat with little temperature change, stabilizing Earth’s climate and organism body temperatures.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Allows capillary action (water moving up plant roots/stems) and supports small insects (water striders).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role\n*   **Medium for Metabolism:** Almost all biochemical reactions (hydrolysis, condensation, photosynthesis, respiration) occur in aqueous solution.\n*   **Transport:** Blood (mostly water) transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, and waste.\n*   **Structure:** Provides turgor pressure in plant cells (keeping them rigid) and acts as a hydrostatic skeleton in worms/jellyfish.\n*   **Temperature Regulation:** Sweating/panting uses water’s high heat of vaporization to cool organisms.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Distribution (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **~71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water.\n*   **97.5% is Saline (Oceans).**\n*   **2.5% is Freshwater.**\n    *   ~68.7% locked in ice caps/glaciers.\n    *   ~30.1% groundwater.\n    *   ~0.3% surface water (lakes, rivers, swamps) + atmospheric water.\n*   **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Continuous movement via evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and runoff.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Use & Significance\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals (irrigation).\n*   **Industry:** ~19% (cooling, processing, manufacturing).\n*   **Domestic:** ~11% (drinking, sanitation, hygiene).\n*   **Energy:** Hydroelectric power; cooling for thermal/nuclear plants.\n*   **Standard:** The **Kelvin** temperature scale and the **kilogram** (originally) were defined based on the properties of water (triple point and mass of 1 dm³ at 4°C).\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water (H₂O) is a polar inorganic compound that is a liquid at standard temperature and pressure. Its unique hydrogen-bonded structure gives it anomalous physical properties—such as a solid phase less dense than its liquid phase, high heat capacity, and universal solvent capabilities—that make it the indispensable solvent and structural medium for terrestrial life and the primary driver of Earth's climate and geology.**"
